Created `/Users/parker/ErrorBoundary.tsx`. Here's a summary of what's implemented:

---

**`ErrorBoundary` class component**

- `getDerivedStateFromError` — stores the error in state, triggering the fallback render.
- `componentDidCatch` — logs a structured object (`message`, `stack`, `componentStack`, `timestamp`) via `console.error`, then calls the optional `onError` prop for external integrations (Sentry, Datadog, etc.).
- `retry()` — resets state to `null`, causing React to re-render the children.

**Props**

| Prop | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `children` | `ReactNode` | The subtree to guard |
| `fallback` | `(error, retry) => ReactNode` | Optional custom fallback renderer |
| `onError` | `(error, errorInfo) => void` | Optional external error reporting hook |

**`DefaultFallback` component** (used when no custom `fallback` is provided)

- Displays the error message and a **Try again** button.
- Has a **Show/Hide details** toggle that reveals the JS stack trace and React component stack.
- Fully accessible via `role="alert"`.

**Usage examples**

```tsx
// Basic — uses built-in fallback UI
<ErrorBoundary>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>

// With custom fallback
<ErrorBoundary fallback={(err, retry) => (
  <div>
    <p>{err.message}</p>
    <button onClick={retry}>Retry</button>
  </div>
)}>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>

// With external error reporting
<ErrorBoundary onError={(err, info) => Sentry.captureException(err, { extra: info })}>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

> **Note:** Error boundaries only catch errors during rendering, in lifecycle methods, and in constructors of the child tree. They do not catch errors in event handlers — use `try/catch` there instead.
